Identify the fundamental, universally applied segments of a triathlon in their proper sequence and describe each portion.

Background:
Although specific regulations may differ among governing organizations (such as World Triathlon or USA Triathlon) and individual race venues, a core set of universal rules exists. Triathlon is traditionally an individual discipline where each participant races against the course and the clock to achieve the fastest time. Consequently, competitors may not receive help from anyone outside the event, except for race‑approved volunteers who provide food and water along the route.

Triathlons are timed across five consecutive phases:

1. From the moment the swim begins until the start of the first transition (swim duration);
2. From the start of the first transition to its conclusion (T1 duration);
3. From the beginning of the bike segment to its finish (cycling duration);
